\end{aligned}

$\therefore$ Current through the circuit, $I_{\mathrm{rms}}=\frac{V_{\mathrm{rms}}}{Z}$
=\frac{25 \sqrt{2}}{500}=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{20} \mathrm{~A}

$\therefore$ Average power dissipated through the circuit is

\begin{aligned}
 < P> & =V_{\text {rms }} \times I_{\text {rms }} \times \cos \phi \\
& =25 \sqrt{2} \times \frac{\sqrt{2}}{20} \times \frac{R}{Z} \\
& =\frac{50}{20} \times \frac{300}{500}=1.5 \mathrm{~W}
\end{aligned}

Key Idea Current amplitude in series LCR circuit is maximum, if there is resonance i.e, $\omega L=\frac{1}{\omega C}$.
Given, $L=0.5 \mathrm{H}, R=10 \Omega, V_{r m s}=200 \mathrm{~V}$ and $f=\frac{150}{\pi} \mathrm{Hz}$
Angular frequency,

\omega=2 \pi f=2 \pi \times \frac{150}{\pi}=300 \mathrm{rad} \mathrm{s}^{-1}

$\because$ For $I_{\text {max }}$ in series $L C R$ circuit,

\begin{array}{rlrl} 
& \omega^2 & =\frac{1}{L C} \\
\Rightarrow \quad C & =\frac{1}{L \omega^2} \\
\Rightarrow C=\frac{1}{0.5 \times 300 \times 300}=22.2 \times 10^{-6} \mathrm{~F}
\end{array}

Current in the circuit,

I_{\text {max }}=\frac{V_{\text {ms }}}{R}=\frac{200}{10}=20 \mathrm{~A}

$\because$ rms voltage difference across the inductor $V_L$ and capacitor $V_C$ are equal. Then

\begin{aligned}
& V_L=I_{\max } \omega L=20 \times 300 \times 0.5 \\
\Rightarrow \quad & V_L=3000 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}

Hence, the correct option is (1).

